## CLI first

This project is generated by rbs. **Anything rbs covers goes through the CLI**, never
through hand-writing files: the CLI lays down the migration, wires the anchors, records
the feature in `[package.metadata.rbs]`, and follows the architecture. The same CRUD
written by hand is seven files, a migration and every anchor to keep in step yourself, for
a module `[package.metadata.rbs]` will go on ignoring.

## This file

Two zones of this file belong to rbs and are regenerated in full: `rbs:guide`, the
handbook you are reading, and `rbs:inventory`, the state of the project. Whatever is
written between their markers is lost, without warning, on the next `rbs upgrade`,
`rbs add` or `rbs generate`. The rest of the file belongs to the project, and the
"Project notes" section at the bottom is there to hold it.

## Commands

| Command | What it does | What it saves you from writing |
|---|---|---|
| `rbs new <name>` | creates a project; `--lang fr\|en` sets this file's language | the whole skeleton |
| `rbs add <feature>` | installs auth, ci, docker, jobs, mail, redis, storage | the feature's wiring |
| `rbs generate crud <name> --fields "..."` | a full CRUD feature | seven files, the seed and the migration |
| `rbs generate feature <name>` | an empty feature | six files |
| `rbs migrate up\|down\|status` | drives migrations | — |
| `rbs migrate new <name>` | an empty migration file | the migration's skeleton |
| `rbs seed` | inserts demo data | — |
| `rbs dev` | services, migrations, a reloaded server | — |
| `rbs doctor` | diagnoses the project | — |
| `rbs upgrade` | aligns the project with the CLI's version | — |

`rbs generate` accepts `--dry-run`: the plan is shown, nothing is written.

## Recipes

- An entity and its CRUD: `rbs generate crud posts --fields "title:string,body:text"`
- A reference to another entity: `--fields "author:references:users"`
- The reverse side of a relation: `rbs generate crud users --has-many posts`
- A feature with no fields: `rbs generate feature webhooks`
- JWT authentication: `rbs add auth`, then copy `RBS_AUTH__SECRET` over
- A hand-written migration: `rbs migrate new add_index_on_slug`

## Enforced architecture

```
src/<name>/  mod · model · dto · repository · service · controller
controller → service → repository → model
```

The dependency is one-directional and strict: a `service` never reaches for
`DatabaseConnection` *itself* — it receives it and hands it to the `repository`, the only
layer that builds a SeaORM query; a `controller` never builds one. `rbs generate crud` adds
a `tests.rs` to those six files and, when the entity can be seeded, the seed
`src/seeds/<name>.rs`. A feature file past ~200 lines is a sign that the feature should be
split.

## Anchors

The CLI never rewrites an AST: it inserts into anchors written as comments. Do not
remove them, do not reorder them, do not write where they sit when a command can do it
instead.

{@ ancres @}

## What rbs does not cover

An endpoint that is not a CRUD, an external HTTP client, a business rule: rbs does not
generate these, and writing them by hand is legitimate. When you do:

- write inside the existing feature, never in a parallel module;
- respect the layers — the controller calls the service, the service calls the
  repository;
- if the code adds a route, register it in `<rbs:routes>` and `<rbs:openapi>` by hand;
- `rbs doctor` reports a module written outside the CLI as a `!` warning, never as a
  failure: it is an observation, and the command still passes.

## Check before you conclude

```bash
cargo fmt --all --check
cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo test --workspace
rbs doctor
```

The tests need the `.env` database running — `docker compose up -d`, or `rbs dev`, which
chains it.
